\section{Typing and Conversion Are Separate Judgments}

In the simply typed lambda calculus, checking an application requires an arrow type and an argument of its domain type. The types themselves do not run programs: after expanding any surface aliases, comparing them is a structural operation. Andrzej S. Murawski's \emph{Typed Lambda Calculi} sets out this starting point and distinguishes typing from operational and denotational semantics. The distinction matters once expressions can occur in types. A type checker may now have to perform part of a computation merely to see that a term has the required type.

Let $\Gamma \vdash t : A$ mean that $t$ has type $A$, and let $\Gamma \vdash A \equiv B$ mean that the well-formed types $A$ and $B$ are \emph{definitionally equal} in $\Gamma$. The latter judgment is a permission to interchange types without building a runtime adapter or supplying a proof term. A typical conversion rule, as in the account by Rob Nederpelt and Herman Geuvers in \emph{Type Theory and Formal Proof}, has the shape
\[
  \frac{\Gamma \vdash t : A\qquad \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B}
       {\Gamma \vdash t : B}.
\]
Here well-formedness of $B$ is included in the equality premise rather than written out separately. Conversion is symmetric; subsumption is not. For instance, width subtyping can let a record with an extra field stand in for one with fewer fields, but it does not make the two record types convertible. Likewise, a proof of an identity type is a term that can be passed to an eliminator; it need not make the endpoints convertible.

\section{Worked Conversion: An Index That Computes}

Suppose a total core has natural numbers and a family $\mathsf{Vec}(A,n)$. Define addition by recursion on its first operand:
\[
  \mathsf{add}(0,n)\ \longrightarrow\ n,\qquad
  \mathsf{add}(S\,m,n)\ \longrightarrow\ S(\mathsf{add}(m,n)).
\]
The checker encounters $v : \mathsf{Vec}(A,\mathsf{add}(0,n))$ where a vector of length $n$ is required. It compares the heads, compares the element types, and reduces $\mathsf{add}(0,n)$ in the index position. The result is literally $n$, even in an open context where $n$ is unknown. By congruence the vector types are convertible. There is no length check in the generated code for this conversion.

Reverse the operands and the same procedure does not establish equality: $\mathsf{add}(n,0)$ is stuck on the unknown $n$. A proof by induction of its equality with $n$ can justify a transport of vectors, but the theorem is not an extra reduction rule. Recursing on the other operand would change which example checks by computation. Choosing definitions is therefore part of designing a usable interface: it affects where clients must supply proofs even when two definitions agree on every closed natural number. The compiler consequences of retaining these indices are pursued in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-148}.

\section{Deciding Equality Without Evaluating Everything}

An implementation can compare weak-head forms, exposing just enough reduction to reveal a constructor, binder, or stuck expression. Matching constructors lead to recursive comparisons of corresponding arguments. To compare two function types, open their codomains with the \emph{same} fresh variable before comparing them. If two expressions are stuck on the same variable, compare their elimination spines rather than guessing what that variable will later denote. Closure environments can delay substitution, and a binder representation based on indices can make renaming invisible to the comparator. User-facing messages should nevertheless retain binder names and original type syntax.

The comparison algorithm owes two distinct facts to the calculus: each reduction it performs must preserve the judgment, and its comparison strategy must recognize every conversion it claims to decide. For a terminating, confluent fragment, comparison of normal forms up to renaming is a useful reference implementation and test oracle. A weak-head algorithm must additionally account for any admitted extensional rules; for example, function $\eta$ can make a variable and its forwarding lambda equal even though their weak-head shapes differ. Do not import such rules from an optimizer: rules valid for total pure terms need not be valid for effectful computation. Unrestricted unfolding of general recursion can also destroy termination. Real checkers make transparency and termination part of their conversion contract; if an implementation runs out of resources checking that contract, it should report that limit rather than claim to have established inequality.
